Use case: stylized-concept. Asset: refined whole-island city setting illustration for an original playful top-down 3D urban sandbox.
Use the four supplied images ONLY as broad starting references: image 1 Brackett provides an island silhouette, working dock end, downtown and university/sports end; image 2 Bellquay provides old port/courtyard character and contrasting warehouse roofs; image 3 Swayhaven provides curving coastal streets, local loops and irregular junctions. REBUILD the city; do not preserve the repeated buildings, bright pale palette, granular texture or rigid grid of the references.
Owner feedback: city needs much more neighbourhood variety, a LARGER high-rise district, more districts, darker and more vibrant colour with freedom beyond Petrol & Coral, far fewer uninterrupted straight roads, more curves and physical dead ends; flat terrain and an island are required.
One landscape image, ideally 1792x1024 or greater. Elevated three-quarter aerial overview framing the ENTIRE island with a visible continuous margin of open water on EVERY side. No land connection. All urban terrain is flat at one common ground elevation, including campus, waterfront and downtown. Subtle irregular shoreline; no hills, cliffs, ramps, bridges, tunnels, elevated roads or other islands joined to it. Whole-city vision, not a six-block diorama and not a gameplay-camera capture.
Style: deliberate smooth stylized 3D art, large clean bevelled forms, saturated pigment, broad readable highlights, simplified roof detail. NOT photorealistic, not a city-builder screenshot, no miniature tilt-shift, grain, noise, pixelation or painted speckle. Retain ordinary recognizable architecture: apartments, shops, offices, warehouses and a campus, not futuristic science-fiction buildings.
Make NINE DISTINCT CITY AREAS VISUALLY LEGIBLE THROUGH GEOMETRY, density, roofs and street pattern, not just colour:
1 a substantial downtown occupying about one fifth of urban land: around two dozen varied conventional tall offices and apartment towers mixed with broad lower podiums and midrise city blocks, not just six skyscrapers;
2 a working dock district with chunky sawtooth warehouses, a few cranes, piers and loading courts;
3 a university quarter near one island end with quadrangles, broad institutional buildings, a library, playing fields and an oval running track;
4 an older port centre of irregular midrise perimeter/courtyard blocks, varied roof outlines, small civic squares and a curving quayside;
5 a compact shopping/entertainment quarter with curved commercial frontage, broad flat-roof halls, a rounded cinema-like building and bold magenta/coral sign SHAPES without lettering;
6 a modern housing estate with separated L-shaped and slab apartment buildings, shared courts and crescent access roads, distinctly unlike downtown;
7 a lower-rise residential area with terraces, courtyard homes and some detached houses, varied roof shapes, staggered blocks and several clear dead-end turning courts;
8 a repair/light-industrial district with long low sheds, service courts, zigzag lanes and vehicle yards, distinctly separate from the shipping port.
Connect these into one inhabited coherent city, not isolated theme-park islands or pie-slice zones. Transition through mixed edges, but do not fill gaps with the same three little houses. Only sparse simple trees; let street layouts and buildings show.
Road character: offset junctions, short rotated grids, bent avenues, coastal curves, T-junctions, crescents and several genuine cul-de-sacs with visible paved turning bulbs/courts. Short access streets can terminate at a warehouse yard, housing court or public square; retain unobstructed alternate pedestrian links and connected main driving circuits. No single straight avenue slicing across the whole island; NO uniform full-city rectangular grid. Ordinary two-way streets with broad continuous sidewalks; no oversized motorways.
Colour and light: significantly darker richer material colours than references, ink-blue and charcoal streets, indigo and deep teal roofs, aubergine/burgundy massing, vivid turquoise, hot coral, magenta, cobalt and amber accents distributed by area. Avoid a sea of pale cream buildings and green roofs. Dark does NOT mean night: fixed moody DAYLIGHT under a cool slate-blue overcast sky, diffuse ambient fill, readable open shadows, no sunset. Windows do not glow as at night. Strong rich local colour, not a grey desaturated scene. Water deep ink teal. Streets quieter than bright small cars/people. No glowing road outlines, no excessive bloom.
No labels, captions, map legend, district boundaries, UI, logos, real brands, explosions, mountains or extra mechanics. This is a broad setting concept.
Composition A: an elongated irregular bean-shaped island running diagonally from the near lower-right docks to the far upper-left university fields, with water visible beyond both tips. The island has broad subtle bulges and recesses, not a rectangle. A generous downtown is slightly off-centre on the right-hand side; old port courtyard blocks and entertainment streets occupy the near left-hand shore; contrasting housing quarters fill the middle-left, and the repair district separates docks from downtown. A pair of curving connected main routes bend around local districts; offset junctions interrupt all long straight sightlines. The university/sports end remains visually clear. Emphasize distinct longitudinal neighbourhood sequences and a big city skyline without losing the whole island.
Additional owner steering, required: image 4 Sunspoke is a supporting reference specifically for LARGE BUILDINGS WITH LARGE SURFACE CAR PARKS. Add a ninth clearly recognizable commercial/retail park district occupying a substantial visible patch, with two or three broad flat-roof big-box stores and/or low office campus buildings, generous open asphalt parking lots with obvious rows of parking bays, small car clusters, planted islands and separate entry/exit access. Make this visible at whole-city scale. Open horizontal parking fields and large simple roofs must strongly contrast with the dense courtyard blocks, high-rise downtown and residential areas. Place the retail park on the near-facing half so it is easy to see, not hidden behind towers. This is one distinct district character, not parking lots spread evenly all over the city. Keep car parks at ground level: no multistorey garages, ramps or roof parking. No real retail brands.

